    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The first few weeks of getting to know someone are exciting, and that excitement can make it hard to see clearly. Learning to notice green flags and red flags helps you slow down, trust your instincts and spend your energy on people who treat you well. It is not about being suspicious of everyone, it is about paying attention.</p>
<p>Below you will find the signs to look for in how someone communicates, respects your boundaries and behaves over time, plus practical steps for what to do when something feels off. These apply to dating, but most also fit new friendships.</p>

<h2>Why early signs are worth noticing</h2>
<p>People usually show you their character in small moments: how they answer a message, how they speak about others, how they react when plans change. Those moments are easier to read at the start, before feelings are deep. Once you are attached, it is much harder to step back and see clearly.</p>
<p>A flag is a signal, not a verdict. A green flag does not promise a perfect relationship, and a red flag does not always mean someone is a bad person. Together, though, they help you decide how fast to move and how much trust to give. Think of them as road signs rather than a final map.</p>

<h2>Green flags to look for</h2>
<p>Healthy early dating usually feels calm, not like a rollercoaster. Look for these signs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent communication.</strong> They reply in a steady, natural way and do not disappear for days and then flood you with attention.</li>
<li><strong>Doing what they say.</strong> If they say they will call at eight, they call, or they let you know if something changes.</li>
<li><strong>Curiosity about you.</strong> They ask questions, remember the answers and bring them up later.</li>
<li><strong>Respect for your boundaries.</strong> When you say no or not yet, they accept it without sulking or pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Honesty about the basics.</strong> Their age, work, family situation and relationship status match what they told you.</li>
<li><strong>Kindness to others.</strong> They treat waiters, drivers, family and friends with courtesy, not only you.</li>
</ul>
<p>A useful test is how you feel after talking to them. If you usually feel relaxed, valued and a bit happier, that is a good sign. If you usually feel anxious or confused, pay attention to that too.</p>

<h2>Red flags to take seriously</h2>
<p>Some signs deserve a pause, and a few deserve an exit. Watch for these:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pushing past your limits.</strong> Pressuring you to meet somewhere private, share personal details or move faster than you want.</li>
<li><strong>Asking for money.</strong> Any request for money, airtime, fees or a loan early on, whatever the reason or emergency.</li>
<li><strong>Stories that do not add up.</strong> Details that change, a life that sounds too perfect or reasons why they cannot video call or meet.</li>
<li><strong>Too much, too soon.</strong> Intense declarations of love within days, or calling you their future spouse before you have met.</li>
<li><strong>Controlling behaviour.</strong> Telling you who to talk to, getting angry when you are busy or demanding to see your phone.</li>
<li><strong>Disrespect.</strong> Mocking you, putting down your friends or speaking badly of every ex.</li>
</ul>
<p>The money one is especially important. Read our guides on <a href="/blog/romance-scams-in-kenya-warning-signs">romance scams in Kenya</a> and <a href="/blog/should-you-send-money-to-someone-you-met-online">whether to send money to someone you met online</a> before you ever consider helping someone you have not met in person.</p>

<h2>Yellow flags and mixed signals</h2>
<p>Not everything fits neatly into green or red. There is a middle area of mixed signals that need a conversation rather than a decision. Examples include a person who is very busy and replies slowly, someone who is shy and takes time to open up, or a person who is still healing from a past relationship.</p>
<p>The way to handle these is to talk about them kindly. You might say, "I have noticed we only talk late at night. Can we plan something earlier in the week?" Their response tells you a lot. A person who listens and adjusts is showing a green flag. A person who gets defensive, mocks you or ignores the request is showing something else.</p>

<h2>Green flags and red flags in how they handle conflict</h2>
<p>You cannot know how someone behaves under pressure until they face a small disagreement. The early weeks usually offer a few chances: a cancelled plan, a misunderstood message, a difference of opinion.</p>
<p>Healthy responses include listening without interrupting, apologising when they are wrong and asking what you need. Unhealthy ones include shouting, going silent for days as punishment, blaming you for everything or turning the argument into a joke. Notice whether you can say "that bothered me" and still feel safe afterwards. If you cannot, that is important information.</p>

<h2>Flags you can spot in messages before you meet</h2>
<p>You can learn a lot from chatting. Watch how the conversation feels over the first week or two. A green flag sounds like "Take your time, I am happy to chat until you are comfortable," or "Would you like to do a video call first?" A red flag sounds like "Give me your number now," "Why are you asking so many questions?" or "I cannot call, but I can tell you I love you."</p>
<p>Pay attention to how someone responds when you slow things down. If you say you would rather meet in a public place, a good person will agree at once. If you say you would prefer not to share your address, a good person will not argue. Someone who sulks, pushes or suddenly cools off when you set a simple limit is telling you something important. Also notice whether the conversation is balanced. Do they ask about you as much as they talk about themselves, or does every chat circle back to their needs?</p>

<h2>Trust your gut, then check the facts</h2>
<p>Your instincts are useful. If something feels off, you do not need a perfect reason before you slow down. At the same time, do not rely on feelings alone. Check what you can. Ask for a video call, see whether their story stays consistent, and talk to a friend who can look at the situation from outside.</p>
<p>Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-verify-someone-is-real-before-meeting">how to verify someone is real before meeting</a> has simple steps, and the <a href="/safety-tips">safety tips</a> page covers what to do before a first meeting. Looking for a verified badge on a profile can also help, as we explain in <a href="/blog/why-profile-verification-matters">why profile verification matters</a>.</p>

<h2>What to do when you spot a flag</h2>
<p>When you notice a red flag, you have four sensible options, depending on how serious it is.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pause.</strong> Slow the pace, take a day to think and do not make big decisions while feeling swept up.</li>
<li><strong>Talk about it.</strong> For small or unclear things, say what you noticed and ask a calm question.</li>
<li><strong>Set a boundary.</strong> Tell them clearly what you will and will not accept, then see whether they respect it.</li>
<li><strong>Leave.</strong> For pressure, lies, controlling behaviour or money requests, step away. You do not owe anyone a long explanation.</li>
</ol>
<p>If someone makes you feel unsafe, stop replying, block them and report them. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-block-and-report-someone-online">how to block and report someone online</a> explains how. Remember that choosing yourself is not rude. It is how you make room for people who deserve your time. When you are ready, <a href="/browse">browse people near you</a> and keep these signs in mind as you chat.</p>
